   183|<h2>Chapter 1 — The Descent Window</h2>
   184|<br/>
   185|<p class="epigraph">Mission AI: "The dust storm is covering the reactor's heat radiators, forcing the Sterling generator offline. I recommend shedding non‑critical loads."</p>
   186|<p class="epigraph">Dr. Arun Patel: "Acknowledged. Initiating shutdown."</p>
   187|<br/>
   188|<p>The hull of the Ares‑One shuddered as the first tendrils of Martian atmosphere slapped against its heat shield. Plasma ribbons licked the viewports, turning the forward cameras into a blinding white wash. Commander Elena Varga gripped her restraints, voice steady over the intercom: "Descent profile nominal. Keep your eyes on the altimeter, Arun."</p>
   189|<br/>
   190|<p>Below them, through a gap in the plasma, Mars revealed itself — not as the rust‑red postcard of imagination, but as something harder and more honest. A plain of ochre dust stretched toward a jagged horizon. Thin wisps of atmosphere curled around debris that had been accumulating for millions of years. Nothing green. Nothing alive. The barometric readout on Elena's cuff climbed: 12 millibars and falling, drawing the crew deeper into a pressure that wanted nothing to do with human lungs.</p>
   191|<br/>
   192|<p>Arun's hands moved across his console without needing to look. Fifteen years of training, four simulation runs this morning alone, and now the numbers dancing at the edge of survivability: peak heating at 1,850 kelvin, dynamic pressure spiking to 1.2 bar, structural loads creeping toward 1.15 gees. He muted the proximity alarm — it had been screaming for the last four minutes — and leaned into his microphone.</p>
   193|<br/>
   194|<p>"We are being squeezed, shaken, and cooked all at once," he said, his tone calm but carrying the weight of everything that could go wrong. "Hold on to something."</p>
   195|<br/>
   196|<p>Ship Two broke through the plasma first. Its beacon flashed green on Elena's display — steady, assured, alive. A surge of relief cracked through her discipline. Then Ship Three appeared, tilted at a wicked fifteen degrees, one landing strut buried past its ankle joint in fine sediment. Its beacon flickered amber. Status: compromised but standing.</p>
   197|<br/>
   198|<p>The descent engine on Ares‑One fired its final correction burn. Elena watched the thrust meter climb and fall in a controlled plume, dust erupting outward in a silent bloom as the lander kissed the surface with a grinding shriek of contact. The landing legs compressed. The frame shuddered. A chunk of thermal tile, shed somewhere in the upper atmosphere, spun away into the haze.</p>
   199|<br/>
   200|<p>Inside, the crew flinched. Then silence. Then the pressure seal beeped its confirmation: 101.3 kilopascals and holding.</p>
